technical_analysis:
**The DTLS record layer has no anti-replay state.** `tls_parse_message()` reads the epoch and sequence number straight out of each received record:

```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=10456 endLineNumber=10462
    if (context->dtls) {
        CHECK_SIZE(buf_pos + 8, buf_len, TLS_NEED_MORE_DATA)
        dtls_epoch = ntohs(*(unsigned short *)&buf[buf_pos]);
        dtls_sequence_number = ntohll(*(uint64_t *)&buf[buf_pos]);

        buf_pos += 8;
    }
```

Those same attacker-supplied values become the AEAD additional data and nonce, so decryption is self-consistent for any record that was ever valid:

```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=10554 endLineNumber=10557
                if (context->dtls)
                    *((uint64_t *)aad) = htonll(dtls_sequence_number);
                else
                    *((uint64_t *)aad) = htonll(context->remote_sequence_number);
```

The value is then stored with no comparison against any previous value:

```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=10787 endLineNumber=10791
    if (context->dtls) {
        context->dtls_epoch_remote = dtls_epoch;
        context->remote_sequence_number = dtls_sequence_number & (((uint64_t)0xFFFFUL << 32) | 0xFFFFFFFFUL);
    } else
        context->remote_sequence_number ++;
```

There is no highest-seen counter, no bitmap or cache of accepted sequence numbers, and no epoch comparison (`grep` for replay/window/highest over `tlse.c` and `tlse.h` returns nothing relevant; `remote_sequence_number` is only used as a nonce input for stream TLS). Once the tag verifies, the record goes to `_private_tls_write_app_data()` (`tlse.c:10823`) and out of `tls_read()`, so the application processes the duplicated message exactly like the original. RFC 6347 section 4.1.2.6 requires the record layer to implement a replay detection window; the README's own warning about the DTLS state machine covers DoS, not replay.

Because the check is absent rather than weak, the attack needs no cryptography: any party that can observe one datagram (an on-path attacker, a shared wireless segment, or the peer itself) can re-inject it at any later point in the connection, and the library reports nothing to the application that would distinguish the copy from a fresh record. The same code path serves the WebRTC/DTLS peer connection shim (`tls_peerconnection_iterate()` feeds datagrams to `tls_consume_stream()`), so data-channel messages are equally replayable.

